## Summary
When `IS_REMOTE_MCP=true`, two concurrent `POST /mcp` requests can leave one of them hung forever. The first request to finish runs its `res.on(\"close\", …)` handler, which calls `server.close()` on a `Server` instance that is shared with every other in-flight request. The transports for the other in-flight requests get severed and their `tools/call` promises never resolve.

## Why this matters for the typical user
This isn't an exotic concurrency edge case — it's the default behaviour of modern AI coding agents. Claude Code is explicitly designed to batch independent tool calls in a single turn, and Cursor agents do the same. The first time an agent inspects more than one thing about a database, it issues two simultaneous `mysql_query` calls and immediately hits this bug.

Downstream we observed an agent fire two parallel `mysql_query` calls, hit this bug, and sit blocked for ~24 minutes waiting for the second tool result that would never come. The Node process stays alive the whole time, so external watchdogs can't see it.

## Affected line
`index.ts`, inside `app.post(\"/mcp\", …)` (line 470 on `main` @ d17bd4e):

\`\`\`ts
const server = mcpServer;   // reuses the module-level singleton
const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });
res.on(\"close\", () => {
  transport.close();
  server.close();           // tears down the SHARED server
});
\`\`\`

The comment immediately above this block already documents the intended

body:
The `connectToHost` method attached error and ready event listeners to SSH clients but never removed them after connection establishment, causing memory leaks and potential runtime errors when post-connection errors attempted to reject already-resolved promises.

**Changes:**
- Refactored anonymous event handlers to named functions that cross-remove each other
- `onReady` removes the error listener when connection succeeds
- `onError` removes the ready listener when connection fails

```typescript
// Before: listeners remained attached indefinitely
client.on('error', (err) => {
  reject(new Error(`SSH connection error: ${err.message}`));
});
client.on('ready', () => {
  resolve(client);
});

// After: listeners clean up after promise settles
const onError = (err: Error) => {
  client.removeListener('ready', onReady);
  reject(new Error(`SSH connection error: ${err.message}`));
};
const onReady = () => {
  client.removeListener('error', onError);
  resolve(client);
};
client.on('error', onError);
client.on('ready', onReady);
```

